WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.159 --> 00:00:02.742
(upbeat music)

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:06.780
- I'm Elina.
- And I'm Sarah.

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:08.450
That was weird.
(laughing)

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:09.513
And I'm Sarah!

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:12.933
And I'm Sarah, and we are Challah Hub.

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:13.880
- [Elina] Today, we're going to show you

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:15.280
how to make a vegan challah.

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:18.350
Challah Hub is a Los Angeles-based delivery company.

00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:20.150
We deliver challah every Friday.

00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:21.814
- [Sarah] Everyone loves it, vegan or not.

00:00:21.814 --> 00:00:23.360
- [Elina] What is challah?

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:27.430
A challah is braided bread traditionally eaten

00:00:27.430 --> 00:00:30.440
on the shabbat table for Jewish people everywhere.

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:32.690
It's the thing that people gather around, every shabbat...

00:00:32.690 --> 00:00:33.900
Shabbat is every Friday.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:36.703
It's meant to be a time of rest and connection.

00:00:36.703 --> 00:00:39.237
(gasping)
(laughing)

00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:40.180
Should we just keep it?
- Just keep it.

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:41.690
- [Elina] Guys, baking is real.

00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:43.560
This is real, this is what happens in life.

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:45.440
- [Sarah] After we had already started Challah Hub,

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:47.440
about six months in, maybe less than that,

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:48.880
I decided to go vegan.

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:51.990
There wasn't any vegan challah options out there,

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:53.940
and there's a lot of vegans out there.

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:56.050
And Elina amazingly was like, okay,

00:00:56.050 --> 00:00:58.270
if we can find a delicious vegan challah recipe.

00:00:58.270 --> 00:00:59.590
So, we tested and tested

00:00:59.590 --> 00:01:01.800
and come up with a really amazing vegan recipe

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:02.930
that we're super proud of.

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:04.370
- [Elina] The thing that's interesting is

00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:05.720
it's a religious bread, right?

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:07.320
It's associated with Judaism,

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:09.360
but a lot of our followers on Instagram

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:10.810
and a lot of our customers are not Jewish,

00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:11.750
and they don't have to be.

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:13.100
If the thing at the center

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:14.960
of your shabbat table is our bread,

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:16.590
then we just think that's the coolest thing ever.

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:18.430
- [Sarah] Really, Elina and I's friendship

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:20.060
has not existed without challah.

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:22.710
The first time we met, it was when I was at a brunch

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:24.240
showing pictures of challah that I had baked,

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:25.322
and Elina was like, 'I'll buy you brunch

00:01:25.322 --> 00:01:27.010
if we bake challah together.'

00:01:27.010 --> 00:01:28.750
I was like, 'deal, I love a free brunch!'

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:30.489
- [Elina] I've been doing so much research

00:01:30.489 --> 00:01:33.480
and looking up so many videos on YouTube.

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:35.650
I had never baked challah before I met Sarah.

00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:37.280
For me, it was kind of like a catch up

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:38.123
when it comes to braiding.

00:01:38.123 --> 00:01:40.040
- [Sarah] Yeah, the student has surpassed the teacher.

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:42.590
I cannot do a six-strand nearly as nice as she can.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:44.060
- [Elina] Sarah has the patience

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:47.350
to create amazing sculptures with challah.

00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:48.746
I have no patience for it.

00:01:48.746 --> 00:01:51.110
- [Sarah] The joke is that I do all of that stuff,

00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:53.640
but then Elina makes sure everything actually gets done.

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:55.480
- [Elina] The business stays a business.

00:01:55.480 --> 00:01:57.880
So, there's a lot of crossover with what we both do,

00:01:57.880 --> 00:01:59.040
but when it comes down to it,

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:00.840
the stuff that I really don't wanna do,

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:02.730
she is really happy doing

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:04.810
and is really good at doing and vice versa.

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:07.720
We're gonna make a four-strand round challah.

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:08.960
- [Sarah] A tic tac toe setup,

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:11.910
and then you're just gonna weave all the way around,

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:13.390
every other strand over,

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:15.643
and then you go back the other direction.

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:16.740
Yeah, and if anything looks weird,

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:19.913
you can just tuck it right under and no one will know.

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:21.660
Really that's what bread making's all about,

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:23.683
hiding the wonky parts.

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:24.563
- [Elina] We're really different.

00:02:24.563 --> 00:02:25.610
Every time we go and do classes,

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:27.060
people are like 'whats the right way to do this?'

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:28.830
And depending on which one of us you ask,

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:29.850
you'll get a different answer.

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:31.689
- It's true.
- Mine's the right one, so...

00:02:31.689 --> 00:02:33.200
(laughing)

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:36.290
- [Sarah] Next we're gonna do a rainbow six-strand challah.

00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:39.730
For braiding, when you're laying out your strands,

00:02:39.730 --> 00:02:43.660
each position is how you count what strand number it is.

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:45.010
- [Elina] If we make colored dough,

00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:48.330
we love using all the natural dyes that are out there

00:02:48.330 --> 00:02:50.910
like matcha, spirulina,

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:53.140
dragon fruit powder, blueberry powder.

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:54.793
There's so much out there.

00:02:54.830 --> 00:02:56.410
- [Sarah] And don't stress if it's not perfect,

00:02:56.410 --> 00:02:57.243
that's the other thing.

00:02:57.243 --> 00:02:58.076
A lot of people are like,

00:02:58.076 --> 00:02:59.980
'oh God, the braid looks so hard and crazy,'

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:01.760
and you know, this one is going to be difficult.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:03.110
- [Elina] Sometimes when you try to connect

00:03:03.110 --> 00:03:04.540
the different colors to each other,

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:06.020
you might have a difficult time,

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:08.290
but we promise that once you bake it it will look great.

00:03:08.290 --> 00:03:09.130
- [Sarah] And no one's gonna complain.

00:03:09.130 --> 00:03:10.520
You're makin' rainbow bread!

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:12.742
But when it comes to actually making challah at home,

00:03:12.742 --> 00:03:15.210
it's artisan, so it can be wonky lookin'

00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:17.910
and that's totally fine.
- We love our wonky challah.

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:19.980
To me, challah means family and friends,

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:21.550
specifically on a Friday night,

00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:24.070
and then, for sure, french toast.

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:25.429
I think that's the best.

00:03:25.429 --> 00:03:27.450
- [Sarah] Don't wrap your challah if it's hot.

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:28.283
No one wants that.

00:03:28.283 --> 00:03:29.116
It's gonna get all soggy.

00:03:29.116 --> 00:03:31.300
Take your challah out of the oven, put it on a cooling rack,

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:32.980
let it chill out, and then once it's cool,

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:34.963
then you can cut into it, rip into it.

00:03:34.970 --> 00:03:37.313
- [Elina] We always have the fight rip or cut.

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:38.203
- And you cut it,
- I'm definitely...

00:03:38.203 --> 00:03:39.380
- You cut the challah.
- No, you rip it.

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:40.980
- [Sarah] That's disgusting, you can't share...

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:42.500
You're going around and...
- What are you talking about?

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:43.333
- [Sarah] It's fine,

00:03:43.333 --> 00:03:44.470
it's like animals getting into the challah.

00:03:44.470 --> 00:03:46.750
- [Elina] I can't enjoy challah when I cut it.

00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:47.627
- Really?
- No.

00:03:47.659 --> 00:03:48.674
- [Sarah] I think you're lying.

00:03:48.674 --> 00:03:49.924
- [Both] Ta da!

00:03:50.535 --> 00:03:52.785
(groaning)

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:55.510
- Oh my God, it's so fluffy!
- So good.

00:03:55.510 --> 00:03:56.710
Can I just see it for a second?

00:03:56.710 --> 00:03:57.873
- Sure.
- Haha!

00:03:57.890 --> 00:03:58.723
- Wait, what?

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:00.202
- [Andrew] Oh, yes.

00:04:00.202 --> 00:04:02.785
(upbeat music)

